    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Machine Learning Model Development & EvaluationObjective\n",
        "\n",
        "The goal of this predictive modeling task is to forecast the next day's All Share Price Index (ASPI) using current daily market indicators such as Turnover, Trading Volume, Number of Trades, Market Capitalization, and the current day's ASPI.\n",
        "\n",
        "Data Splitting:\n",
        "\n",
        "Since this is time-series data, a chronological train-test split was used (shuffle=False). $80\\%$ of the historical data was used to train the models, and the most recent $20\\%$ was held out for testing.\n",
        "\n",
        "Algorithms Compared:\n",
        "\n",
        "1.Linear Regression:\n",
        "\n",
        "A basic yet highly interpretable model that assumes a linear relationship between the input features and the target variable.\n",
        "\n",
        "2.Random Forest Regressor: An advanced ensemble learning method that builds multiple decision trees to capture non-linear and complex patterns in the data.\n",
        "\n",
        "Model Evaluation & Justification:\n",
        "\n",
        "The models were evaluated using Mean Absolute Error (MAE) to measure the average prediction error, and the R-squared ($R^2$) Score to measure how well the variance of the target variable is explained by the model.\n",
        "\n",
        "*Linear Regression Results: Achieved an $R^2$ score of approximately 0.99.\n",
        "\n",
        "*Random Forest Results: Achieved a lower $R^2$ score compared to the Linear model.\n",
        "\n",
        "Justification of the Best Model:\n",
        "\n",
        "Based on the evaluation metrics, Linear Regression is selected as the best-performing model. In time-series financial data like this, the next day's ASPI is heavily dependent on the current day's ASPI (a linear step). Random Forest models often struggle to extrapolate values outside the training data range (e.g., when predicting new all-time market highs), whereas Linear Regression effectively captures this underlying linear trend. Therefore, Linear Regression is the most suitable algorithm for our interactive dashboard implementation."
      ],
